======================================================================================================================
L33 -- is a MOND-scalar cone thousands of times wider than the light cone admissible?
======================================================================================================================

======================================================================================================================
A -- CONTROL: the quadratic action of the MOND scalar, expanded from THE_ACTION sections 1-3
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] A0 [CONTROL] the carried kernel's saturation point and ceiling reproduce THE_ACTION section 3 (s_sat = 2.540, C = 0.6476)   (s_sat = 2.5396, C = Delta_max = 0.64761)

  A1  Expansion (flat metric, preferred frame n^mu = (1,0,0,0), background static with V along x).
      L_2 / (eps^2)  =  J_Y*K_B*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), x)**2 + J_Y*K_B*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), y)**2 + J_Y*K_B*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), z)**2 - 2*J_Y*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), x)**2 - 2*J_Y*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), y)**2 - 2*J_Y*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), z)**2 + 2*J_YY*K_B*g_phi**2*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), x)**2 - 4*J_YY*g_phi**2*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), x)**2 + K_2*Derivative(f(t, x, y, z), t)**2
      kinetic coefficient (d_t f)^2 : K_2
      longitudinal stiffness (d_x f)^2: -J_Y*K_B + 2*J_Y - 2*J_YY*K_B*g_phi**2 + 4*J_YY*g_phi**2
      transverse   stiffness (d_y f)^2: J_Y*(2 - K_B)   (d_z f)^2: J_Y*(2 - K_B)
  [PASS] A1 [CONTROL] the quadratic action expanded here gives the acoustic metric G^{mu nu} = |K_2| n^mu n^nu - (2-K_B)[J_Y q^{mu nu} + 2 J_YY V^mu V^nu], i.e. kinetic |K_2|, transverse (2-K_B)J_Y, longitudinal (2-K_B)(J_Y + 2Y J_YY)   (all four coefficients match the covariant form term by term; the AeST mixing 2(2-K_B)J^mu d_mu phi carries one time derivative and is antisymmetric, so it does not enter this Hessian (L13's finding, used here, not re-derived))

  A2  The longitudinal stiffness in terms of the kernel.  With the static law J_Y(g_phi) g_phi = g_N,
      g_phi = a_0 Delta(s), g_N = a_0 s and Y = g_phi^2:
      J_Y + 2 Y J_YY  =  1/Derivative(Delta(s), s)
  [PASS] A2 [CONTROL] the longitudinal stiffness is exactly 1/Delta'(s) for EVERY kernel -- i.e. it is the inverse of the boost function's slope, which is what the bounded-boost theorem constrains   (J_Y + 2 Y J_YY = 1/Delta'(s), symbolically, for a generic Delta; a_0 cancels)

  A3  c_s,perp/c = sqrt((2-K_B) J_Y / |K_2|), independently of L13's code.
