======================================================================================================================
B -- does this action have a genuine preferred foliation, and is the scalar's cone spacelike for it?
======================================================================================================================
  B1  THE_ACTION section 1 defines the clock through a SCALAR tau: n_mu = -d_mu tau / sqrt(-(d tau)^2),
      with NO independent aether vector.  Hypersurface-orthogonality is then an identity, not a
      solution property (unlike Einstein-aether).  Frobenius test: n ^ dn = 0 for a generic tau.
  [PASS] B1 [CONTROL] the clock defines a genuine preferred FOLIATION: n built from the scalar tau is hypersurface-orthogonal identically (n ^ dn = 0 for a generic tau), so tau = const is a global time function and not a special solution   (all four components of eps^{mu nu rho sig} n_nu grad_rho n_sig vanish symbolically)

  B2  Is the leaf tau = const SPACELIKE for the scalar's cone?  The condition is that the leaf
      conormal n_mu be timelike for the inverse acoustic metric:  G^{mu nu} n_mu n_nu > 0.
      G^{mu nu} n_mu n_nu = K2   -- because q^{mu nu} n_nu = 0 and V.n = 0 BY CONSTRUCTION
      This is |K_2| for EVERY J_Y and J_YY: the result does not depend on the cone's width at all.
      numerically, at every environment above and both footings: G^{mu nu}n_mu n_nu = |K_2| = 5.0e+05 > 0 (and 2.0e+05 at the window's other edge)
  [PASS] B2 the tau = const leaves are SPACELIKE with respect to the MOND scalar's characteristic cone at c_s = 2.5e3 c -- so the cone is causally consistent with the preferred foliation   (G^{mu nu}n_mu n_nu = |K_2| > 0 identically, independent of J_Y, J_YY and hence of c_s.  The no-ghost condition |K_2| > 0 IS the spacelike condition; nothing else enters)

  B3  NEGATIVE CONTROL -- the test must be able to fail.  Replace the scalar's projector q by the
      projector qtilde orthogonal to a DIFFERENT unit timelike vector ntilde, boosted by v relative
      to the clock (this is what a second preferred frame in the action would do).  Then
          qtilde^{mu nu} n_mu n_nu = -1 + (ntilde . n)^2 = gamma^2 - 1,
      so G^{mu nu}n_mu n_nu = |K_2| - (2-K_B) J_Y (gamma^2 - 1), which turns NEGATIVE at
          gamma^2 - 1 > |K_2|/[(2-K_B) J_Y] = 1/c_s^2   <=>   v > c/c_s.
      environment                             footing       c_s/c   boost margin v [km/s]
      Saturn orbit                          canonical       1.958               1.531e+05
      Saturn orbit                                alt       1.784                1.68e+05
      Earth orbit (1 AU)                    canonical       18.76               1.598e+04
      Earth orbit (1 AU)                          alt        17.1               1.754e+04
      Cassini conjunction, b = 1.6 R_sun    canonical        2522                   118.9
      Cassini conjunction, b = 1.6 R_sun          alt        2298                   130.5
      explicit sign flip at Cassini conjunction (|K_2| = 5e5, J_Y = 1.767e+12):
        v = 0.9 c/c_s =    107.0 km/s : G^{mu nu}n_mu n_nu = +9.5000e+04  (spacelike)
        v = 1.1 c/c_s =    130.8 km/s : G^{mu nu}n_mu n_nu = -1.0500e+05  (TIMELIKE -> closed causal curves)
  [PASS] B3 [CONTROL, negative] the spacelike test HAS TEETH: a mode whose cone is tied to any frame boosted relative to the clock fails it, and the margin is finite and computable   (the leaves stop being spacelike once the scalar's own frame is misaligned from the clock's by v > c/c_s; the tightest margin above is 119 km/s (Cassini conjunction), SMALLER than the Solar System's 370 km/s motion relative to the CMB.  The theory is safe only because Q = n.d phi and V = q.d phi are built from the SAME tau, so the alignment is exact by construction, not by tuning)

  B4  No closed causal curve.  Along a characteristic of a mode of finite speed c_s, in the
      preferred frame, dtau/dlambda = k^0 > 0 strictly: tau is a strictly monotone function of the
      curve parameter, so no causal curve of the scalar can close.  Quantitatively, the leaf-crossing
      rate of the fastest ray is dtau/d(proper distance along the leaf) = 1/c_s > 0:
        canonical: c_s/c = 2522  =>  dtau/dx = 1/(c_s) = 1.323e-12 s/m  > 0  (finite, non-zero)
              alt: c_s/c = 2298  =>  dtau/dx = 1/(c_s) = 1.452e-12 s/m  > 0  (finite, non-zero)
  [PASS] B4 no closed causal curve exists for the scalar at c_s = 2.5e3 c: tau increases strictly along every characteristic, because c_s is FINITE   (1/c_s = 1.323e-12 s/m at the widest cone -- non-zero, so tau is strictly monotone. The condition is finiteness, not subluminality; see D2 for where finiteness fails)

======================================================================================================================
C -- the universal horizon: what a c_s = 2.5e3 c mode can and cannot escape
======================================================================================================================
  Built from scratch, in the regime this action actually sits in: all c_i <= 1e-5, so the metric is
  Schwarzschild to O(1e-5) and the clock is a TEST field on it.  With the c_2 (khronometric lambda)
  term dominant the khronon equation makes the leaves CONSTANT MEAN CURVATURE surfaces; the maximal
  (K = 0) family of Schwarzschild is the textbook case, and its LIMITING leaf is the universal
  horizon.  (Estabrook et al. 1973 for the slicing; Barausse-Jacobson-Sotiriou 2011 and
  Blas-Sibiryakov 2011 for the identification.  Existence of a universal horizon for GENERAL c_i is
  a literature result, cited, not re-derived; the exact example below is derived here.)

  C1  leaf normal u and in-leaf radial unit vector s:  u.u = -1,  s.s = 1,  u.s = 0
  [PASS] C1 [CONTROL] the constant-mean-curvature leaf's unit normal u (u^r = C/r^2) and the in-leaf radial unit vector s (s^r = sqrt(g), g = f + C^2/r^4) satisfy u.u = -1, s.s = +1, u.s = 0 exactly   (verified symbolically in Schwarzschild coordinates)

  C2  limiting leaf (double root of g):  r_UH = 3*M/2,  C = 3*sqrt(3)*M**2/4,  g''(r_UH) = 16/(9*M**2)
      the metric horizon is at r = 2M, so r_UH = 3/4 of it, INSIDE it.
  [PASS] C2 [CONTROL] the limiting leaf of the maximal foliation of Schwarzschild sits at r = 3M/2 with C = 3 sqrt(3) M^2 / 4, reproducing the published universal-horizon radius   (r_UH = 3M/2 (Estabrook et al. 1973's limiting maximal slice; the universal horizon of Barausse-Jacobson-Sotiriou 2011 / Blas-Sibiryakov 2011), g''(r_UH) = 16/(9 M^2))

  C3  A ray of speed c_s in the CLOCK frame has tangent k = u + c_s s.  It moves outward iff
      k^r = -|C|/r^2 + c_s sqrt(g) > 0  (infalling clock, C < 0), i.e. iff
            g(r)  >  C^2 / (c_s^2 r^4).
      at c_s = 1 (light) this reduces to f(r) = 0, i.e. r = [2*M] -- the METRIC horizon.
  [PASS] C3 [CONTROL] the escape condition returns the METRIC horizon r = 2M exactly when c_s = 1, so the machinery is anchored to a known answer   (g(r) = C^2/r^4  <=>  f(r) = 0  <=>  r = 2M)

  C4         c_s/c     r_horizon / M    (r_h - r_UH)/M    fraction of the way in
                 1      2.0000000000        5.0000e-01              0.0000000000
                10      1.5596531365        5.9653e-02              0.8806937269
             18.76      1.5321737182        3.2174e-02              0.9356525635
               100      1.5061071450        6.1071e-03              0.9877857100
              2522      1.5002427966        2.4280e-04              0.9995144069
             1e+04      1.5000612356        6.1236e-05              0.9998775288
             1e+06      1.5000006123        6.1227e-07              0.9999987755
             1e+09      1.5000000056        5.5713e-09              0.9999999889
  [PASS] C4 the c_s = 2.5e3 c mode is TRAPPED: its horizon lies strictly between the universal horizon r = 3M/2 and the metric horizon r = 2M, so a black hole is a black hole for it too   (r_h(c_s = 2522) = 1.50024280 M, i.e. 0.0486% of the way from the universal horizon to the metric horizon; r_h(c_s = 18.8) = 1.532174 M)

  C5  Analytically, near the double root g ~ (8/9)(r - r_UH)^2/M^2 and C^2/r^4 -> 1/3, so
          r_h - r_UH = sqrt(3/8) M / c_s  ->  0   as c_s -> infinity.
      predicted 2.428228e-04 M at c_s = 2522; measured 2.427966e-04 M (ratio 0.9999)
  [PASS] C5 an INFINITE-speed mode is trapped too: r_h(c_s) -> r_UH exactly, so the universal horizon censors arbitrarily fast modes and black-hole thermodynamics is not defeated by the scalar's cone   (r_h - r_UH = sqrt(3/8) M/c_s, verified to 0.01%; at c_s = 1e9, r_h - r_UH = 5.57e-09 M.  This is the resolution of Dubovsky-Sibiryakov's perpetuum-mobile problem (hep-th/0603158) supplied by Berglund-Bhattacharyya-Mattingly (arXiv:1202.4497, arXiv:1210.4940): the universal horizon carries its own surface gravity and temperature, common to all speeds)

======================================================================================================================
D -- the initial value problem
======================================================================================================================
  D1  Transverse sector.  omega^2 = c_s,perp^2 k^2 with c_s,perp^2 = (2-K_B)J_Y/|K_2| > 0 and the
      energy density |K_2|(d_t f)^2 + (2-K_B)J_Y |grad_perp f|^2 positive definite.  Real, distinct
      characteristics; strongly hyperbolic; finite domain of dependence of half-angle arctan(c_s).
  [PASS] D1 the transverse sector is strongly hyperbolic on every background sampled (real characteristic speeds, positive-definite energy), so it has a well-posed Cauchy problem on the leaves   (c_s,perp^2 > 0 and finite at all 12 (environment, footing) points; K_B = 0.2 < 2 and |K_2| > 0 are the two conditions)

  D2  Longitudinal sector, ON THE ACTION AS PUBLISHED.  A2 showed the longitudinal stiffness is
      (2-K_B)/Delta'(s).  The carried kernel is EXACTLY flat beyond s_sat = 2.540 (THE_ACTION s.3),
      so Delta'(s) = 0 identically at every Solar-System background and
          c_s,par^2 = (2-K_B) / (Delta'(s) |K_2|)  =  INFINITY.
      An infinite characteristic speed is not a closed causal curve (tau never DEcreases, it stays
      constant along the ray), but it is not hyperbolic either: the longitudinal evolution equation
      degenerates into an elliptic CONSTRAINT on the leaf, to be solved with boundary conditions at
      infinity.  That is the khronometric 'instantaneous mode' (Blas-Pujolas-Sibiryakov 2011), and it
      is evolved in the literature as a mixed elliptic-hyperbolic system (arXiv:1512.04899), not as a
      Cauchy problem with a finite domain of dependence.
                     s      Delta(s)    Delta_prime(s)       c_s,par/c
                 1e+05      0.647610               0.0        infinity
                 1e+07      0.647610               0.0        infinity
            6.3343e+07      0.647610               0.0        infinity
            1.1441e+12      0.647610               0.0        infinity
  [FAIL] D2 the initial value problem is well posed AS THE ACTION IS PUBLISHED, i.e. every propagating direction has a finite characteristic speed on the Solar-System background   (it is not: Delta'(s) = 0 exactly for s > 2.540, so the LONGITUDINAL scalar speed is infinite, not 2.5e3 c.  The system there is elliptic-hyperbolic, not hyperbolic.  This is the same missing input L13's P9 identified from the other side (no C^2 kernel => no cubic action): a C^2 continuation of Delta past its maximum with Delta' > 0.  NOTE the direction: this makes the cone WIDER than P8's number, never narrower)

  D3  CONTROL -- the same machinery must detect a genuine ill-posedness.  Set K_B > 2 (so the
      gradient stiffness (2-K_B)J_Y < 0): omega^2 < 0, exponential growth at every k, ill-posed.
      K_B = 0.2: omega^2/k^2 = +352.12 c^2   (healthy)
      K_B = 3.0: omega^2/k^2 = -195.62 c^2   (gradient instability, growth rate ~ c k sqrt|.|)
  [PASS] D3 [CONTROL] the hyperbolicity test is not blind: it flags a gradient instability when one is put in by hand (K_B > 2)   (omega^2/k^2 = -195.6 c^2 < 0 at K_B = 3.0, against 352.1 c^2 at K_B = 0.2)

  D4  The coherence operator.  With xi^2 |grad_perp V|^2 in the action the dispersion becomes
      omega^2 = c_s^2 k^2 (1 + xi^2 k^2) (Lifshitz z = 2): omega is real at every k, so the Cauchy
      problem is still well posed in the Lifshitz sense, but the GROUP velocity is unbounded --
      there is no cone at all in the UV and hence no finite domain of dependence.
          footing   xi [pc]     v_g at k = 1/xi     v_g at k = 1/AU    v_g at k = 1/(1 m)
        canonical      0.10               39.81           7.741e+05             1.158e+17
              alt      0.15               36.27           1.058e+06             1.583e+17
  [FAIL] D4 the coherence operator leaves the mode with a bounded group velocity, i.e. a finite domain of dependence survives in the ultraviolet   (it does not: v_g ~ 2 c_s xi k grows without bound (Lifshitz z = 2).  This is a KNOWN and accepted feature of the Horava-type host, not a new pathology -- omega(k) is real at every k so the Cauchy problem is well posed -- but it means the answer to 'how wide is the cone' is 'there is no cone', and the c_s = 2.5e3 c figure is a long-wavelength (k << 1/xi) statement only)

======================================================================================================================
E -- does ANY existing observation bound this scalar's speed?
======================================================================================================================
  E1  GW170817.  The bound |c_T/c - 1| < ~1e-15 is on the TENSOR sector.  In this action
      c_T^2 = 1/(1 - c_13) and c_13 = c_1 + c_3 = K_B - K_B = 0 exactly, so c_T = c identically and
      independently of |K_2|, J_Y and the kernel.  The MOND scalar is a spin-0 mode: it does not
      enter the tensor dispersion at all.
      c_T^2 = 1/(1 - c_13)  ->  at c_13 = 0: 1; d(c_T^2)/d|K_2| = 0, d(c_T^2)/dJ_Y = 0
  [PASS] E1 [CONTROL] GW170817 constrains only the tensor speed, which this action fixes to c exactly and independently of the scalar sector -- so it places NO bound on c_s   (c_T^2 = 1/(1 - c_13) = 1 at c_13 = 0; the scalar's |K_2| and J_Y do not appear in it)

  E2  Gravitational Cherenkov, KINEMATICS.  A particle of energy E, mass m, speed v emitting a
      quantum of speed c_s: energy-momentum conservation gives
          k = 2 (p - E c_s) / (1 - c_s^2).
      k = (-2*E*c_s + 2*p)/(1 - c_s**2)
      For c_s > 1 the numerator p - E c_s < 0 (since p < E always) and the denominator 1 - c_s^2 < 0,
      so k > 0 formally -- but the emitted quantum must also satisfy k <= p for a real final state.
      Check the threshold directly:  emission requires v > c_s, and v < 1 < c_s.  No solution.
  [PASS] E2 no gravitational-Cherenkov channel EXISTS into a superluminal mode: the threshold v > c_s cannot be met by any particle, since v < c < c_s.  Superluminality is the SAFE side of the Cherenkov bound   (Elliott, Moore & Stoica (JHEP 0508:066, hep-ph/0505211) constrain aether modes to be at or ABOVE c for exactly this reason; the repo's own g03v applies the same requirement to the khronon.  A cone wider than the light cone is not merely tolerated by this bound, it is what the bound asks for)

  E3  The flip side, computed rather than assumed: the SAME formula makes the scalar SUBLUMINAL at
      low acceleration.  The crossing is at c_s = c, i.e. J_Y = |K_2|/(2-K_B):
           |K_2|    J_Y at c_s = c        s_crit    g_N,crit [m/s^2]      r from the Sun
         2.0e+05        1.1111e+05         71957          6.7365e-06            29.67 AU
         5.0e+05        2.7778e+05    1.7989e+05          1.6841e-05            18.76 AU
      Galactic ambient acceleration is ~a_0 (s ~ 1), where c_s/c = 0.002487 at |K_2| = 5e5 -- i.e. ~746 km/s.
      So the mode is superluminal only INSIDE ~19 AU of the Sun and subluminal everywhere else.
  [PASS] E3 [diagnostic] the superluminal cone is a LOCAL, high-acceleration phenomenon: the same law makes the mode deeply subluminal in the interstellar medium, which is the corner the Cherenkov bound actually constrains   (c_s = c at s = 1.8e+05 (|K_2| = 5e5), i.e. within 19 AU of the Sun; at galactic ambient acceleration c_s = 746 km/s.  Reported as a diagnostic, not as a PASS/FAIL, because it is the opposite corner from this lane's question)

  E4  Whether THAT subluminal corner is excluded is a rate question, and this lane does not compute
      the rate (lane L19 owns the rate machinery, for a different mode in a different action).  What
      is computed here is the GEOMETRY that sets the cutoff, because it is what differs between the
      two lanes.  Milgrom, PRD 83, 084005 (2011), arXiv:1102.1818: radiation of wavenumber k is
      generated at distance ~1/k from the primary, where the PRIMARY's own field sets the local
      acceleration; in a theory that becomes GR at high acceleration this cuts the emission off at
      k_cut = 1/r_M, giving D_loss = q c^2/a_0 with the cosmic-ray energy cancelling exactly (L19
      re-derived that published result independently; it is cited here, not re-derived).
      In THIS action the emission switches off EARLIER than Milgrom's r_M -- at the smaller radius
      r_c where the cone crosses c -- because inside r_c there is no channel at all (E2):
            E [eV]    footing       r_M [m]       r_c [m]     r_M/r_c   D_loss [m] (worst case)     /10 kpc
           1.0e+20  canonical     1.127e-08     2.658e-11       424.1                 1.067e+22       34.59
           1.0e+20        alt     1.027e-08     2.422e-11       424.1                 8.859e+21       28.71
           3.0e+20  canonical     1.953e-08     4.604e-11       424.1                 1.067e+22       34.59
           3.0e+20        alt     1.779e-08     4.194e-11       424.1                 8.859e+21       28.71
      Two suppressions are DELIBERATELY omitted from that worst case, both of which lengthen D_loss:
      (i) between r_c and r_M the scalar is screened, J_Y = s/Delta >> 1, so its coupling to the
          source is suppressed by ~1/J_Y there -- and that is exactly the shell doing the emitting;
      (ii) the scalar's kinetic normalisation is |K_2| = 5e5, i.e. the canonically normalised field
          carries a 1/sqrt(|K_2|) coupling, suppressing the rate by a further ~1/|K_2|.
      Neither is computed here.  The number below is therefore an upper bound on the loss.
  [PASS] E4 [worst case] even with the emission cut off only where the cone crosses c -- a factor s_crit = |K_2| Delta_max/(2-K_B) EARLIER than Milgrom's own MOND-radius cutoff -- and with the coupling taken at FULL gravitational strength (no 1/|K_2| suppression), the loss distance still exceeds the 10 kpc Galactic path used by Moore & Nelson   (D_loss = 2 (c^2/a_0)/s_crit = 1.07e+22 m (35x the Galactic path, canonical; 29x alt) at |K_2| = 5e5.  ASSUMPTIONS, stated: the rate formula is L19's verified transcription of Milgrom eq. (4), the coupling is taken as gravitational-strength (an UPPER bound on the rate -- the scalar's normalisation |K_2| = 5e5 suppresses it further, by a factor that is NOT computed here), and the path is Galactic.  For an EXTRAGALACTIC path of 100 Mpc this same worst case would fail by 289x, so the margin is not large: this is the one empirical item this lane leaves open, and the missing input is the scalar-matter vertex normalisation)

  E5  Everything else that has been claimed to bound a gravitational-sector speed:
      * binary pulsars (Yagi, Blas, Yunes, Barausse, PRL 112, 161101, arXiv:1307.6219) bound the
        preferred-frame parameters alpha_1, alpha_2 and the dipole COUPLING -- not c_s.  A larger
        c_s SUPPRESSES scalar radiation (the flux falls with the mode speed), so the superluminal
        cone makes those bounds easier, not harder.
      * the CMB and growth of structure constrain the scalar's speed in the COSMOLOGICAL background,
        where J_Y is small and the mode is deeply subluminal (c_* ~ 400 km/s); they say nothing
        about the Solar-System value.
      * no time-of-flight measurement of this mode exists or can exist with present technique: the
        scalar is not coupled to matter directly (matter is minimally coupled to g alone,
        requirement 11), so there is no emitter and no receiver.
  [PASS] E5 [verdict on observation] no existing observation is violated by c_s = 2.5e3 c -- and, stated plainly, no existing observation BOUNDS this scalar's speed from above at all   (GW170817 constrains the tensor; Cherenkov constrains slow modes and is turned OFF by superluminality; pulsars constrain couplings; the CMB constrains the cosmological (subluminal) value.  The Solar-System scalar cone is an untested prediction, in the strict sense that nothing measures it)

======================================================================================================================
F -- is the superluminal cone forced, or is it a kernel choice?
======================================================================================================================
  F1  The kernel-free form.  The static law of ANY matter-sourced scalar of this class is
      div[J_Y grad phi] = 4 pi G rho, hence J_Y g_phi = g_N exactly, hence
          c_s,perp^2 / c^2  =  (2 - K_B) g_N / ( g_phi |K_2| ).
      a_0 does NOT appear.  The two footings give IDENTICAL numbers for this statement; the footing
      enters only through which g_phi a given kernel produces.
